From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Mon, 27 Jul 2026 07:45:45 +0200
Subject: Classify opaque aliases over mutable types under capture checking

An opaque alias over Array (or a Stateful class) previously lost its
mutability classification outside its defining scope: the classification
recursion falls through to the alias's upper bound, so plain parameters
of such a type were untracked pure values that subsumed into exclusive
receivers, silently bypassing the read-only discipline. The recursion
now pierces opaque aliases via translucentSuperType, so an alias over a
mutable type is tracked exactly like the type it hides.

The stdlib's IArray alias stays excluded, matching its iarraypure
treatment: aliases over IArray classify as immutable, both directly and
when IArray is reached by dealiasing inside a defining scope.

Developed and tested with LLM assistance (Claude, Anthropic), reviewed
by hand.

Enabled by `-Z:opaque-mutability`; without it, upstream's code runs at every site this
patch touches.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Proscala-Patch: iarraypure-mutalias
---
 compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/cc/CaptureOps.scala | 3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

diff --git a/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/cc/CaptureOps.scala b/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/cc/CaptureOps.scala
index 24ff282573c7312e5a63b9a048db94a0b058d224..b0bec25c79ed0a45aa6424d1d7146a6913d8a450 100644
--- a/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/cc/CaptureOps.scala
+++ b/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/cc/CaptureOps.scala
@@ -430,6 +430,9 @@ extension (tp: Type)
       val sym = tp.typeSymbol
       if sym.isClass
       then (if sym.isArrayUnderStrictMut then defn.Caps_Mutable else sym).derivesFrom(cls)
+      else if sym.isOpaqueAlias && !(sym eq defn.IArrayAlias)
+          && config.Proscala.enabled(config.Proscala.OpaqueMutability)
+      then tp.translucentSuperType.derivesFromCapTrait(cls)
       else tp.superType.derivesFromCapTrait(cls)
     case tp: (TypeProxy & ValueType) =>
       tp.superType.derivesFromCapTrait(cls)
